speaker 1: I see OCI split into five categories in the diagram - is this Oracle's official taxonomy?
speaker 2: The official service list is much more granular. We grouped things into compute, storage, networking, database, and security based on what's most commonly used and easiest to map to AWS.
speaker 1: These five categories happen to be exactly the modules I use every day in AWS architectures too.
speaker 2: Right, so you'll find an old AWS friend in almost every category.
speaker 1: What specific services fall under each category?
speaker 2: Compute includes Compute, Bare Metal, GPU/HPC, OKE, Functions, and Container Instances. Storage covers Object, Block, File, and Archive storage.
speaker 1: What about networking and database?
speaker 2: Networking's core is VCN, Load Balancer, and FastConnect. For database, we focus on Autonomous Database and HeatWave MySQL - the two flagship services Oracle wants you to remember most.
speaker 1: Security sounds pretty abstract.
speaker 2: We focus security on IAM and Compartment, since that's where OCI differs most from AWS IAM.
speaker 1: Is the learning order just a flat walkthrough of these five, or is there a sequence?
speaker 2: There's a sequence, following how you'd actually build an architecture - compute first, then storage, then networking, then database, and security last.
speaker 1: That matches how a real project goes too - spin up servers first, then wire up storage and networking.
speaker 2: Exactly, so once you finish the course you can go build one hands-on right away.
